{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/464325-scion-2e-how-will-the-mortal-tier-change-the-world-of-scion","id":464325,"title":"[Scion 2e] How will the mortal tier change the world of scion?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-31T23:17:16-07:00","id":464325,"title":"[Scion 2e] How will the mortal tier change the world of scion?","content":"A few things Immediately jump to mind for me...<br /> <br /> -The mythological world will have to have so more room for real mortal involvement. Things like fate binding and the sheer above and beyond power of mythological beings could make it difficult for mortal to really influence it under their own agency. I would bet mortals will be able to come away away from mythological interactions, perhaps not unchanged, but more often having managed to find themselves on the winning side of that conflict.<br /> <br /> -I am looking forward to a slightly more fleshed our world of lesser mythological beings, the sort of people like sorceresses, nymphs, fauns, and minor spirits who might pass as mortals. Or the strange forgotten beasts who are mostly mundane but none the less rare, dangerous, fantastic, and maybe just a little magic.<br /> <br /> -One thing I have been thinking about is it might be fun if playing a scion starts in the Mortal book with small almost unnoticeable boons that represent the very beginnings of a Scion discovering their heritage while they are still mostly mortal.<br /> <br /> How do you think the world of Scion will change on the lower end of play for its second edition?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1522-turkeygiant","name":"turkeygiant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1522_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"999"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T01:20:43-07:00","id":464400,"title":"","content":"I'm hesitant to say the &quot;world&quot; of Scion is really going to change because of this. Making the pre-visitation urban fantasy level of play open doesn't really require any sort of changes to the general conceit of Scion. What it requires is mechanics that adequately support this mode of play along with transitioning to higher tiers without falling to pieces.<br /> <br /> Everything in the Origins tier is <i>supposed</i> to be part of Scion already. It just wasn't really supported well in 1e.<br /> <br /> I think the play experience will improve drastically because these elements will be relevant to the game instead of the setting saying they were supposed to be relevant and then not realizing it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T06:31:40-07:00","id":464625,"title":"","content":"I think mortal involvement is a good thing. I had the uncomfortable feeling that many thought human casualties could be simply tied to collateral damage <i>&quot;It's war, and there are casualties. And if a human needs to be sacrificed for the greater good...&quot;</i> I don't think that kind of indifference is a good thing. Humans are a part of this reality and this is where half of the scion originates and how s/he is reared. And I would hope 2e reflects that mortals can affect change in some way. And if a scion takes an indifferent attitude towards human life then there should be consequences. And if those consequences put mortals on the opposing side then so be it.<br /> Even more interesting however is if the mortal scion takes an opposing view to his/her pantheon. Having a liaison of a sort might help. It would be rather mind blowing to have mythological beings suddenly spring up without some sort of preparation before meeting the upper echelons of the supernatural community. Of course there might be some explanation as to why an early scion had certain psychological issues, or why s/he had certain predilections or attitudes that could never be explained...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T09:34:05-07:00","id":464749,"title":"","content":"The thing that I am eagerly looking forward to Scion: Origins is the chance to start out pre-Visitation. At my table, we have a play style, as all tables do. We enjoy starting out as people who could or should get trashed by the setting's main antagonists and then working their way up. In Exalted, for example, we start out pre-Exaltation and then we will Exalt. So the chance to do so with Scion is pretty great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T10:22:47-07:00","id":464838,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/464325-scion-2e-how-will-the-mortal-tier-change-the-world-of-scion?p=464400#post464400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm hesitant to say the &quot;world&quot; of Scion is really going to change because of this. Making the pre-visitation urban fantasy level of play open doesn't really require any sort of changes to the general conceit of Scion. What it requires is mechanics that adequately support this mode of play along with transitioning to higher tiers without falling to pieces.<br /> <br /> Everything in the Origins tier is <i>supposed</i> to be part of Scion already. It just wasn't really supported well in 1e.<br /> <br /> I think the play experience will improve drastically because these elements will be relevant to the game instead of the setting saying they were supposed to be relevant and then not realizing it.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good point the &quot;world&quot; of scion doesn't really need to be changed that much, you just need to see more of that lore that was supposed to exist represented in the rules. Though even if the big lore doesn't need to be changed I could see there being some slight tweaks to introduce more primarily mortal organisations, and maybe some near mortal races of myth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1522-turkeygiant","name":"turkeygiant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1522_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"999"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-06T18:44:22-07:00","id":470571,"title":"","content":"I think it becomes more reflective of the source material. Mortal or mostly mortals like Odysseus who had to use their wits and occasionally a nifty artifact to circumvent the plans of the gods for their lives or at shift them to a course with a bit more agency are as much a part of myth as continent rending half gods like Heracles or the cosmic Game of Thrones saga of the Olympians. All have their place. The worlds of Scion are growing to encompass the adventures of the rest of the Argonauts or the great warriors of the Iliad which mean Scions will seem greater because there's more compelling points of reference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1881-the-cowardly-scion","name":"The Cowardly Scion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1881_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}